# CableLIMS reverse proxy -- terminates HTTPS in front of the app (see
# DEPLOYMENT.md step 7). Copy this file next to caddy.exe and edit the one
# placeholder below before running it.
#
# Why a static address instead of Caddy's "on-demand TLS" (which would
# issue a certificate for whatever hostname a client asks for, without
# editing this file at all): on-demand TLS needs its own permission-check
# configuration to avoid being a cert-issuance-abuse vector, which only
# matters for a server reachable from the open internet. This is a LAN-only
# appliance reached by a small, known set of lab devices at one fixed
# address (DEPLOYMENT.md step 1 already has you get a static IP or DHCP
# reservation for exactly this reason) -- a static site block naming that
# one address is simpler and has nothing extra to configure or secure.
#
# Why `tls internal` (Caddy's own local CA) rather than a real
# publicly-trusted certificate (Let's Encrypt etc.): those require a public
# domain name Caddy can prove control of via a DNS or HTTP challenge --
# there isn't one here, this server only has a private LAN IP. `tls
# internal` is the standard answer for exactly this shape of deployment.
# The one operational cost is that each lab device needs Caddy's root
# certificate installed once to avoid a browser warning -- see
# DEPLOYMENT.md step 7 for how.

# EDIT THIS: replace with this server's actual LAN IP or hostname (the one
# you looked up with `ipconfig` and got a static IP / DHCP reservation for).
192.168.1.50, localhost {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000

	# B4 (Review #2 remediation): tell browsers to always use HTTPS for this
	# address from now on, even if someone types the bare http:// address or
	# an old bookmark. max-age is 1 year -- long, deliberately: HSTS's whole
	# value is that it protects the very first connection attempt too, which
	# a short max-age would keep re-exposing.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
}

# Plain HTTP on the same address redirects to HTTPS, rather than serving
# anything -- so a bookmark or typed address without "https://" still ends
# up secure instead of silently staying on cleartext port 80.
http://192.168.1.50, http://localhost {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
